// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row toplevel_refbind_over_ref_scrutinee_segv (tier 1, `run 139`).
// FOUND 2026-09-09e by an abuse-direction hand program written as the LEGAL control for
// the Rust-2024 binding-modifier round, not by any probe.
//
// A TOP-LEVEL `ref w` binder over a REFERENCE-TYPED scrutinee. This is legal in Rust and
// in Logos's own spec: `pat.binding.default-by-ref-mode` shifts the default binding mode
// only when a NON-reference pattern is matched THROUGH the reference, so at the top level
// the mode is still `move` and a written `ref` is legal. `build_pattern_impl`'s
// PAT_WILD+IS_REF door agrees and types the binding `&&i64` — sema PROVES it does, because
// `let a: i64 = *w;` is refused with "expected i64, got &i64", i.e. one deref of `w` is a
// `&i64` exactly as `&&i64` requires.
//
// CODEGEN DOES NOT AGREE. It binds `w` to the reference VALUE rather than to the
// reference's ADDRESS, so `*w` yields the i64 payload 3 and `**w` dereferences 3 AS AN
// ADDRESS. Exit code 139 (SIGSEGV). Same family as 26eacf7bf, "a place built on a
// reference-typed local addressed the binding's own eight bytes, not the referent".
//
// MEASURED on base 6e10b5ffbceba8b4 43 and on 77beab6f631806d5 43: compiles rc 0, runs 139.
// The one-level shape `match n { ref w => *w }` over a NON-reference scrutinee is correct
// (exit 0), and `match &o { ref w => }` that never READS through the binding also exits 0 —
// so the defect is specifically the deref of a ref-bind whose pointee is itself a
// reference, and the read is what exposes it.
//
// The oracle is an EXIT CODE and not a stdout string: 0 when the two derefs read the 3
// that is there, 1 if they read something else, 139 today.
package toplevel_refbind_over_ref_scrutinee_segv;
fn main() -> i32 {
    let n: i64 = 3i64;
    match &n {
        ref w => {
            let b: &i64 = *w;
            let a: i64 = *b;
            if a != 3i64 { return 1i32; }
            return 0i32;
        }
    }
}
